Title:
OWN-Defender — Windows Security Center COM Research

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
OWN-Defender is a Windows internals/reverse-engineering project that documents how Windows Security Center (WSC) represents and registers antivirus products via COM interfaces. It traces the full execution chain from COM activation (CLSID/IID, CoCreateInstance, QueryInterface, ATL interface maps, vtables) through WSCAPI.dll into an RPC call boundary (e.g., NdrClientCall3) that reaches the Security Center service. The repo includes a small C++ implementation (EXE + DLL) used to reproduce and verify the registration behavior and observe results via WMI (ROOT\SecurityCenter2\AntiVirusProduct). A key theme is validating assumptions (GUID names, decompiler output, method signatures) by correlating interface definitions, vtable layouts, assembly, and runtime behavior. It’s useful for red teamers and malware analysts studying WSC registration mechanics, and for blue teams/detection engineers wanting to understand and monitor “fake AV” registration patterns. The work emphasizes that WSC product registration is not inherently equivalent to disabling Microsoft Defender, but it can affect how security posture is reported.

Technical Focus:
- Windows Security Center (WSC) ISV COM interfaces (CLSID_WscIsv, IWscAVStatus4)
- COM internals: CoCreateInstance, QueryInterface, IID/CLSID mapping, vtables, reference counting
- ATL interface maps and _ATL_INTMAP_ENTRY resolution
- Reverse engineering methodology (IDA/Ghidra limitations, thunks/wrappers, calling conventions)
- WSCAPI.dll call chain and Windows RPC (NdrClientCall3, MIDL stubs)
- Runtime verification via WMI (ROOT\SecurityCenter2)

Use Cases:
- Reverse engineer and validate how AV products are registered/represented in WSC
- Build lab PoCs to study “security product spoofing” and its telemetry footprint
- Develop detections for suspicious WSC registration activity and related persistence mechanisms
- Understand COM/RPC boundaries in Windows security architecture for research and auditing

Keywords:
Windows Security Center, WSC, WSCAPI.dll, COM, CLSID_WscIsv, IWscAVStatus4, CoCreateInstance, QueryInterface, ATL, interface map, vtable, GUID, IID, RPC, NdrClientCall3, MIDL, ROOT\\SecurityCenter2, AntiVirusProduct, reverse engineering, DefendNot